<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Doha is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, on the east coast of the Qatar peninsula. A small pearling settlement in the 19th century, it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-thani">Al Thani</a> family, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-thani">Muhammad bin Thani</a> signed an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> there in 1868. Oil and gas revenues turned it into a modern city, and it has hosted major diplomatic talks, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doha-agreement-2020">US–Taliban agreement</a> of 2020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doha.jpg" alt="Doha" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex Sergeev (www.asergeev.com) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pearl_Marina_in_Nov_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Doha stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast in the central-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, at an elevation of about 10 metres, north of Al Wakrah and south of Al Khor and Lusail. Its name comes from the Arabic dohat, meaning roundness, a reference to the rounded bays along the coast. The climate is hot desert, with long, extremely hot summers, short mild winters and very little rain, mostly between October and March. Land reclamation off the coast has added 400 hectares of land and 30 kilometres of coastline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Doha began in the 1820s as an offshoot of the older settlement of Al Bidda. The earliest documented mention of Al Bidda dates from 1681, in an account by the Carmelite Convent, and in 1765 the German explorer Carsten Niebuhr mapped it as Guttur. In January 1823 the British political resident John MacLeod visited Al Bidda to meet Buhur bin Jubrun, chief of the Al-Buainain tribe and the initial founder of Doha, and noted that it was the only substantial trading port on the peninsula. Written records often conflated the two settlements because they lay so close together.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-thani">Al Thani</a> family migrated to Doha from Fuwayrit shortly after Bin Tarif's death in 1847 and in the following years took control of the town, at various times switching allegiance between the Al Khalifa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and the Saudis. Pearling became central to the economy, and the population grew to about 12,000 in the first half of the 20th century. In 1907 the city accommodated 350 pearling boats with crews of 6,300 men. That year the pearl market collapsed, and the aftermath led to the country's first custom house in Doha.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> ratified its first constitution in April 1970 and became independent in 1971, when Doha was declared the capital. The University of Qatar opened in 1973 and the Qatar National Museum in 1975. In the 1970s the old neighbourhoods were razed and their inhabitants moved to new suburbs such as Al Rayyan, Madinat Khalifa and Al Gharafa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Doha is the capital, largest city and main financial hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, and over 80% of the country's population live in the city or its suburbs. Its population is overwhelmingly composed of expatriates, with Qatari citizens a minority, and most residents are Muslim. The metropolitan area includes Education City, devoted to research and education, and Doha Sports City, which includes Khalifa International Stadium. The city hosted the 2006 Asian Games and the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Doha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shastri published the manuscript's 47 padas with the commentary through the Bangiya Sahitya Parishad in 1916, as part of his Hajar Bacharer Purano Bangala Bhasay Bauddhagan O <b>Doha</b> (&quot;Buddhist Songs and Couplets&quot;), under the title Charyacharyavinishchayah.</blockquote><a href="/songs/charyapada">Charyapada</a></li><li><blockquote>After a twenty-year insurgency against the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan and NATO forces, and the US withdrawal agreed at <b>Doha</b> in 2020, the Taliban seized Kabul on 15 August 2021 and restored the Islamic Emirate.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan (2021-present)</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman troops arrived in <b>Doha</b> in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Sheikhdom of Qatar</a></li></ul></section>
